From 8c532dd60fcb0ff14ec00921cb23d10437c88a44 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Michal Ruprich <michalruprich@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 7 Sep 2026 11:21:49 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] syscall: use O_PATH for held directory traversal

The 3.5.0 path-confinement rework routes every directory traversal
through openat(..., O_RDONLY | O_DIRECTORY).  Opening a directory
O_RDONLY needs read permission, but traversing one needs only search
('x'), so the confinement made rsync fail on directories it had always
been able to work through: a search-only (0111) parent, and -- the case
that actually bites -- a write/search-only (0333) "drop box"
destination, where the receiver's change_dir() and its temp file both
died with EACCES.  Stock 3.2.7 handles both.

Use O_PATH on Linux for descriptors held only for pathname traversal,
fchdir(), or as *at() authority, keeping the O_RDONLY fallback for
platforms without it.  Because an O_PATH fd cannot be read or
fdopendir()'d, every place that previously handed a traversal fd back
to a caller now reopens the endpoint with the caller's requested access
mode: ona_open()'s O_DIRECTORY leaf, and the three secure_walk_at()
endpoints that used to ds_take() the walk's own fd.  The sites that
enumerate directory contents -- secure_opendir() in flist.c above all
-- deliberately keep O_RDONLY | O_DIRECTORY, so refusing to list an
unreadable directory is unchanged.

New helpers name the distinction at the call site:
secure_relative_dirfd(), secure_relative_dirfd_at_beneath() and
open_no_attacker_symlinks_dirfd() return traversal authority;
secure_relative_open() keeps returning an endpoint with the requested
access.

Beyond the upstream change, three resolvers that this branch carries in
an older shape need the same treatment or the fix stops at the
destination directory: the held-dirfd cache anchor in dpc_dir_fd()
(upstream reaches it via open_anchor_dirfd()), and secure_mkstemp()'s
inline component walk, which resolves --temp-dir itself.

Upstream: 3b1eb8dd7fb8ad9d39dc666f0decd737cc3b8146 (#1052)
Upstream: 240bd9df961afcd27cd496b52d9f526b73b0dfd5 (#1065)
Fixes: https://github.com/RsyncProject/rsync/issues/1064
